{#
  Who We Are. Composes the frame and the locked components; copy lives in front
  matter. Three notes for review:

  1. NO alt-glyph on any headline here. The handoff swaps the A in the "The
     Team" h2, but design.md section 3 requires each swap to be justified by
     what that specific word MEANS, and a crossbar-less A says nothing about a
     team. It was available, not meaningful, so it is dropped.

     The one swap that remains is the A in "Circulation" in the name-origin
     list. That is not a headline swap: it is the wordmark itself, quoted as
     the term being defined, and design.md section 9 locks the crossbar-less A
     into the wordmark in every setting. Rendering it plain there would show a
     wordmark the brand does not use.

  2. This page carries Alex Loveless's FULL testimonial, the relationship
     account. The home page carries the short pull quote drawn from it. They
     are different treatments of the same source and must not be swapped or
     duplicated.

  3. The bilingual notes in three team members' roles are the team's language
     capability, which is real and stays. It describes the PEOPLE. It is not a
     signal that the site should be bilingual; see the language decision in
     SITE_ARCHITECTURE.md.
#}
{% from "components/container.njk" import container %}
{% from "components/section.njk" import section %}
{% from "components/button.njk" import button %}

{#- THE 88px RHYTHM. This page has been reset around the retired above-title, so
    every section step here is 22 on Tailwind's scale, which is 88px, rather than
    24, which is 96. section() takes it as an argument because the macro is
    shared by five pages and the other four have not moved yet. -#}
{#- THE SECTIONS, NAMED FOR THE HEADER'S LEFT SLOT.
    ============================================================

    data-orient is read by main.js, which caches each one's top and writes the
    name of whichever is under the bar into the masthead. Declared rather than
    discovered: three of these five blocks have no heading to read a name from,
    so scraping the DOM would have named two sections and shrugged at the rest.

    THESE ARE THE ABOVE-TITLES, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE CHANGE.
    "In a client's words" and "Why the name" were the labels removed from this
    page in the previous commit. They are not gone from the site; they moved to
    the one place that can carry orientation without repeating it beside the
    thing it describes. The page reads without them and the header answers where
    you are. -#}
{% call section(pad="py-22", orient="Who We Are") %}
  <h1 class="cs-opening__headline">{{ opening.headline }}</h1>
  <p class="cs-opening__lede">{{ opening.lede }}</p>
{% endcall %}

{% call container(class="pb-22", orient="The Team") %}
  <h2 class="cs-teamhead">The Team</h2>
  <ul class="cs-teamgrid">
    {% for member in team %}
      <li class="cs-member">
        {# Portrait ground stays blank until real photography lands. Adding a
           `photo` to this member in front matter swaps the image in with no
           markup change. #}
        <div class="cs-portrait">
          {% if member.photo %}
            <img class="cs-portrait__img"
                 src="{{ site.cdn }}{{ member.photo }}"
                 alt="{{ member.name }}, {{ member.role }}"
                 width="340" height="400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
          {% endif %}
          <span class="cs-portrait__accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="cs-member__specialty">{{ member.specialty }}</p>
        <p class="cs-member__name">{{ member.name }}</p>
        <p class="cs-member__role">{{ member.role }}</p>
        <p class="cs-member__bio">{{ member.bio }}</p>
      </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

<div class="cs-graft">
  <div class="cs-rule cs-rule--graft cs-rule--graft-l" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="cs-rule cs-rule--graft cs-rule--graft-r" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  {% call container(class="relative") %}
    <img class="cs-graft__figure"
         src="{{ site.cdn }}cs-img-graft-square.webp"
         alt="Graft, two stems joined into one stronger stem"
         width="1120" height="560" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <div class="cs-graft__tick" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  {% endcall %}
</div>

{#- ABOVE-TITLE RETIRED. This block opened with "In a client's words", which
    told the reader what a blockquote with a cite in it already tells them. The
    testimonial's own top margin went with it: that margin existed to hold the
    label off the quote, and with no label above it the quote sits on the
    container's padding instead. See who-we-are.css. -#}
{% call container(narrow=true, class="pt-22", orient="In a client’s words") %}
  <blockquote class="cs-testimonial">
    <p>{{ testimonial.body }}</p>
    <cite>{{ testimonial.cite }}</cite>
  </blockquote>
{% endcall %}

{#- ABOVE-TITLE RETIRED. "Why the name" sat centred over a definition list whose
    two terms are Circulation and Studio, which is the answer to the question the
    label was asking. The list's top margin went with it, for the same reason as
    the testimonial above. -#}
{% call container(narrow=true, class="py-22", orient="Why the name") %}
  <dl class="cs-origin">
    {% for entry in origin %}
      <div class="cs-origin__group">
        <dt class="cs-origin__term">
          {%- if entry.term == "Circulation" -%}
            Circul<span class="alt-soft">a</span>tion
          {%- else -%}
            {{ entry.term }}
          {%- endif -%}
        </dt>
        <dd class="cs-origin__def">{{ entry.definition }}</dd>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </dl>
{% endcall %}

{#- cs-who-close carries this page's 88px step. .cs-close's own padding is
    --space-96 and it is shared with Results, so it is overridden here rather
    than changed there. Both move to the token in the commit that resets the
    remaining pages, and this class comes out then. -#}
{#- NOT A NAMED SECTION. The close is a call to action, not a place the reader
    is in, and naming it would have the label answer "Start a conversation" to
    the question "where am I". It is also the last block on the page, and a
    final block shorter than the viewport can never pass under the bar: measured
    here, it begins at 3434 while the page stops scrolling at 3120. The clamp in
    main.js means the last NAMED section is always reachable; this one is simply
    not one. -#}
{% call container(class="cs-close cs-who-close") %}
  <p class="cs-close__note">{{ close }}</p>
  {{ button("Start a conversation", href="/contact/") }}
{% endcall %}
